1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,070
Failed call. Deducted: zero testnet NEAR.

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:14,500
An agent needs one document checked before it files it.
It pays 0.1 testnet NEAR — but only if the check comes back valid.

3
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:23,720
The buyer prepays testnet NEAR and sets a spending limit in the contract.

4
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:36,950
One successful call. The contract deducts exactly one call price —
only because the buyer verified the success receipt.

5
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:55,950
Now a failed call. The buyer claims the failure —
the held 0.1 testnet NEAR is released, never deducted.

6
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:02,550
No receipt in thirty seconds — same release, never deducted.

7
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:09,900
The receipt didn't check out, so nothing was deducted.

8
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:16,960
Second call, budget now used.

9
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:37,410
And over budget, the contract refuses the call before any money moves.
The contract logged the refusal; balance unchanged.

10
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,750
The contract holds the deposit and refuses overspending on-chain.

11
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:55,660
Would you trust an agent that only pays for work it can prove?
Replay it — repo in the replies — and tell us.

